L45: # Dynamic Nested Hierarchies: Pioneering Self-Evolution in Machine Learning Architectures for Lifelong Intelligence
L46: Akbar Anbar Jafari Affiliation: Institute of Technology Affiliation: University of Tartu Affiliation: Tartu, Estonia Email: akbar.anbar.jafari@ut.ee    Cagri Ozcinar Affiliation: Institute of Technology Affiliation: University of Tartu Affiliation: Tartu, Estonia Email: chagri.ozchinar@ut.ee    Gholamreza Anbarjafari Affiliation: 3S Holding OÜ Affiliation: Tartu, Estonia Email: shb@3sholding.com
L190: In this section, we provide rigorous mathematical proofs and theoretical analyses for the key claims regarding DNH. We focus on establishing convergence guarantees under non-stationary data distributions, proving enhanced expressivity over static NL paradigms [cite36†4 ], and deriving bounds on adaptation efficiency. All proofs are self-contained, with assumptions clearly stated, and build upon the formulations introduced in Sections 2 and 3.
L191: We employ tools from optimization theory, graph theory, and stochastic processes to ensure technical precision.
L192: ### 4.1 Assumptions and Preliminaries
L193: 
L194: To facilitate the analyses, we outline the necessary assumptions and recall key definitions.
L195: Assumption 1 (Lipschitz Continuity and Smoothness). The loss functions $\mathcal{L}^{(1)}$, $\tilde{\mathcal{L}}^{(\ell)}$, and $\mathcal{L}_{\text{meta}}$ are $L$-Lipschitz continuous and $\beta$-smooth, i.e., $\|\nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta)-\nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta^{\prime})\|\leq\beta\|\theta-\theta^{\prime}\|$ and $\|\nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta)\|\leq L$ for all $\theta,\theta^{\prime}$.
L196: Assumption 2 (Bounded Distribution Shifts). The data distribution $p_{t}(\mathbf{x})$ evolves with bounded shifts, measured by the total variation distance: $\sup_{t}d_{\text{TV}}(p_{t},p_{t-1})\leq\delta$ for some $\delta>0$.
L197: 
L198: Assumption 3 (Graph Stability). The DAG $\mathcal{G}_{t}$ has bounded degree $d_{\max}$ and diameter $D_{\max}$, ensuring computational tractability in gradient flows.
L199: Recall that a DNH model optimizes the meta-loss $\mathcal{L}_{\text{meta}}$ over a dynamic hierarchy $\mathcal{G}_{t}$ with levels $L_{t}$ and frequencies $\{f^{(\ell)}_{t}\}$. The evolution is governed by $\mathcal{G}_{t+1}=\arg\min_{\mathcal{G}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{meta}}(\mathcal{G};\mathcal{G}_{t},\mathbf{x}_{t},\Delta_{t})$.
